e value) — hmm, exact equality depends on identical construction. My test-local construction must be byte-identical in geometry to the example's. I'll write it carefully. Risk: if not exactly equal, test fails and I'd need the repair pass. Safer: assert equality within a tiny tolerance like 1e-12? The fixture used exact equality and passed, so construction-identical runs are deterministic. But my control differs from the example in material object only (true elastic vs curve) — fixture's true_elastic case also replaced material via `model.add_material(\"steel\", 2.1e11, 0.3, density=7850.0)` AFTER _add_ep_material — wait no: `_preyield_case(coupling, scale, true_elastic=True)` calls `_build_ep_model(coupling, scale)` (which adds the EP material) and then adds \"steel\" again with plain elastic. So add_material with duplicate name overwrites! Great — so my test can do:\n\n```python\ncase = build_model(\"interpolated\")\ncase.model.add_material(\"steel\", 2.1e11, 0.3, density=7850.0)\nresult = solve_static_nonlinear(... same controls ...)\n```\n\nand compare probe uz to the payload's probe_displacement. But wait — does overwriting the material after elements were created matter? Elements reference material by name (\"steel\"), so they resolve at solve time. The fixture did exactly this and it worked (elastic case completed, matched exactly).\n\nHmm, but the fixture's true_elastic case asserts get_material(\"steel\").hardening_curve is None — so overwrite works.\n\nBut careful: the fixture's exact-equality assertion was between plastic(scale 1.0) and elastic(scale 1e4) and separately true_elastic — `test_preyield_plastic_matches_distinct_linear_elastic_material` computes displacement_error = norm(u_p-u_e)/norm(u_e) and asserts... let me check what bound it uses. It compares physical stresses with tolerances. The first test asserts exact array equality between scale 1.0 and scale 1e4 (both with hardening curves). For true_elastic, displacement_error is computed — let me