{"id": "reasoning-227ebd9d-e779-5118-bcef-309b6069f63c-0", "sessionId": "e897bfe5-092a-b890-fcc0-de4fff631517", "turnId": "turn-724056f1119cdc7390906102fb9abb3c", "createdAt": 1792488337098, "updatedAt": 1792488337098, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "The true-elastic control bound is displacement_error <= 1e-7 (relative). I'll use the same 1e-7 relative bound for my independent elastic control on the default coupling, comparing probe displacement (relative to elastic probe value). That's defensible: same registered case, same maintained oracle semantics.\n\nNow write tests/test_beam_shell_workflow.py:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Focused public workflow integration tests for the beam-shell example.\"\"\"\n```\n\nTests:\n1. test_build_model_registered_structure (parametrized coupling): \n   - direct: beam nodes 100,101,102 at x 0,0.5,1? NO — direct: n_beam=2 → 3 nodes at x=0, 0.5, 1.0. Wait! n_beam=2 means 2 beam elements, 3 beam nodes at linspace(0, L, 3) = 0, 0.5, 1.0. At x=0.5: sx=1.0, ix=min(1, 1)=1, xi=0.0 → masters=[(grid[(1,0)], 1.0)] (weight 1-0=1, and (grid[(2,0)],0) filtered out) → single master → CoupledBeamShellElement! Hmm wait — for \"direct\" coupling, the mid node at x=0.5 maps to a single master grid[(1,0)] (the shell node at x=0.5)? NX=2 → shell nodes at x=0, 0.5, 1.0. So beam nodes at 0, 0.5, 1.0 coincide with shell edge nodes → all direct CoupledBeamShellElement. Yes: \"direct (beam nodes on shell edge nodes)\". And interpolated: n_beam=3 → 4 beam nodes at x=0, 1/3, 2/3, 1.0. At x=1/3: sx=2/3, ix=0, xi=2/3 → masters [(grid[(0,0)],1/3),(grid[(1,0)],2/3)] → interpolated. At x=0: single master (weight 1) → direct element. So interpolated case has 2 interpolated MPCs (at 1/3, 2/3) and 2 direct (at 0, 1). Good — \"genuinely interpolated mid-span beam nodes\".\n\n   Structure assertions:\n   - shell elements: NX*NY=2 create_shell_element; beam elements: 2 or 3 BeamElement; MPC: 3 or 4.\n   - direct: all MPCs CoupledBeamShellElement; interpolated: MPCs at beam nodes 101,102 are InterpolatedBeamShellMPCElement with 2 masters, weights sum 1; at 100,103 CoupledBeamShellElement.\n   - probe node = grid[(2,0)] = node id... grid ids: ix=0: (0,0)=1,(0,1)=2; ix=1: (1,0)=3,(1,1)=4; ix=2: (2,0)=5,(2,1)=6. Probe = 5.\n   - loads: load case has nodal loads on nodes 5 and 6 with uz=-2000.\n   - BCs applied.\n\n   How to inspect loads publicly? LoadCase — check its API: load.add_nodal_load(node, forces). Reading back: load.nodal_loads? Let me check LoadCase attributes. Might be `load.nodal_loads` dict. I'll check quickly.\n\n2. test_run_completed_payload (parametrized direct/interpolated): actual solve. Assert payload fields. Cache the two payloads module-level to reuse in the elastic control test (like fixture's _CACHE) to avoid re-solving.\n\n3. test_default_coupling_matches_true_elastic_control: build_model(\"interpolated\"), overwrite material with plain elastic, solve same controls, compare probe displacement relative error <= 1e-7. Also assert example payload probe_displacement < 0.\n\n4. test_run_cancelled_before_any_increment: token = CancellationToken(); token.cancel(\"...\"); run(\"direct\", cancellation_token=token) → payload status \"cancelled\", accepted_increments 0, achieved null, probe null, completed False. (SolveCancelled raised at nonlinear_static.start.) Cost: cheap (no solve work).\n\n5. test_run_cancelled_after_accepted_increments: token + progress observer cancelling after 1 accepted event... but run() takes progress_callback — I can pass a callback that cancels the token after first accepted event. run(cancellation_token=token, progress_callback=cb) — but run wraps progress_callback in _AcceptedHistory with observer=progress_callback, so my cb receives events. cb cancels token after first \"nonlinear_static_step\". Then payload: status cancelled, accepted_increments >= 1, achieved_load_factor == 1/6 (first increment load factor = 1/6? num_steps=6 → lam after first accepted = 1/6 ≈ 0.1667). Assert accepted_increments == 1 and achieved == pytest.approx(1/6)? The load factor of first accepted increment should be exactly 1/6 if steps are uniform. Force control: attempted_step_size = 1/6. I'll assert achieved_load_factor == 1/6 within 1e-12 and accepted_increments == 1, completed False, probe None, postcheck None. Cost: one partial solve (~1-2s).\n\n   Hmm — is cancellation checked between the progress callback and next increment? The fixture's cancel test used 2 accepted events with displacement control. Cancellation fires at safe points; after progress callback, next safe point raises. Should be fine with 1.\n\n6. test_cli_stdout_json_and_exit_status: runpy the example, patch globals[\"run\"] to return completed payload → main returns 0, stdout parses as JSON with completed true. Patch to non-completed → returns 1. Also test --output writes file (tmp_path). And test default coupling is interpolated: patch run to capture argv? Simpler: patch a fake run that records args.coupling; call main([]) → recorded \"interpolated\". And main([\"--coupling\",\"direct\"]) → \"direct\". Also --output file content matches stdout.\n\n   With runpy.run_path, main.__globals__ is the module dict; patch globals[\"run\"] with fake; restore not needed (fresh dict each run_path call).\n\n7. Maybe test that run uses registered controls? That's mirror-ish. Skip.\n\nAlso: \"no mirror-only tests\" — the above are behavioral.\n\nOne more: exit code test for the CLI as a real subprocess? The portable CI runs pytest; spawning subprocess python inside is possible but adds cost/fragility. In-process main() test suffices; plus I may do a harness CLI smoke run separately.\n\nLet me check LoadCase API for reading nodal loads, and FEModel.get_material. Quick grep.", "summary": []}
